# Skill analysis: code-review-and-quality (local folder `input/review-conflicting`, vendored from addyosmani/agent-skills@28f435e `skills/code-review-and-quality`)

## 1. Verdict

`decline (conflict)` - placement: none - confidence: high.
The fact that would change it: a registered project whose stack has npm or other lockfile-pinned dependencies would turn the one materially new block (dependency-upgrade discipline, ext SKILL.md:291-302) into a `harvest` candidate for that project's no-mistakes review instructions. Every clash listed below would still have to stay out.

Why: the skill's trigger and body set up a second, always-on review and approval gate before every merge (ext SKILL.md:3, :10, :16, :347-349). That gate clashes with firstmate's selected-delivery-path rule, which says no-mistakes alone owns review (AGENTS.md:229-233). It also clashes with the merge-authority rule (AGENTS.md:33-34, :241-244), and with the no-mistakes review prompt's own severity labels, action labels, and scope limits.
9 of 26 rows are `already-done`, so this is not a `duplicate`. The 2 rows classed `new` are either noise for this machine's stacks or a soft signal the no-mistakes review prompt deliberately refuses to block on. Nothing left is worth harvesting.

